      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright 2001 S. Karger AG, Basel",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
            "content": "To compare the effect of a calcium antagonist and a beta-blocker on left-ventricular diastolic function in patients with ischemic heart disease."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "138 patients with chronic stable angina pectoris were randomized in a multicenter, double-blind trial to treatment with either mibefradil or atenolol for 6 weeks (50 mg once daily for 2 weeks followed by 100 mg once daily for 4 weeks). The ratio between early (E) and late (A) diastolic mitral flow velocities (E/A), the E wave deceleration time (DT) and the left ventricular isovolumetric relaxation time (IRT) were measured by Doppler echocardiography as parameters of left-ventricular diastolic function initially, after 4 and after 6 weeks of treatment."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Mibefradil did not change the E/A ratio significantly (+4%, NS), while atenolol treatment resulted in a significant increase in the E/A ratio (+20%, p < 0.001). Mibefradil treatment, on the other hand, resulted in a significant decrease (-8%, p < 0.001) in IRT, while atenolol treatment did not change IRT. Neither mibefradil nor atenolol treatment changed DT significantly."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "Both mibefradil and atenolol treatment significantly improves echocardiographic indices of left-ventricular diastolic function in patients with chronic stable angina. However, they affect different parameters and thus apparently act through different mechanisms."
          }
        ]
      },
